\DOC ORTHOGONAL_COMPONENTS_TAC

\TYPE {ORTHOGONAL_COMPONENTS_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Proves that two state components are orthogonal, closing the goal.

\DESCRIBE
Takes no arguments. Applied to a goal whose conclusion asserts orthogonality of
two state-component expressions {t} and {u} (registers, flags, or memory
components built with {:>}, byte/bytelist/sized-word views at computed
addresses), it closes the goal:
{
   A ?- orthogonal_components t u
   ========================================  ORTHOGONAL_COMPONENTS_TAC
}
\noindent The assumptions {A} play no visible role and are not altered.

\FAILURE
Observed to fail on an orthogonality goal whose two components are a
sized-memory view and a byte-sized memory view at addresses not in the shapes
the tactic can decompose; the failure is a term-destruction error raised inside
the tactic while taking apart the component terms, not an argument error.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces only this behaviour appears: either the orthogonality
goal is closed or an exception is raised; no trace shows the tactic returning a
transformed goal.

\ENDDOC
